# Holocron .gitattributes
#
# THIS FILE MUST BE IN THE FIRST COMMIT. Attributes are applied at `git add` and
# `git checkout` time, never retroactively. Landing it later means an isolated
# `git add --renormalize .` commit to repair the tree, tangled into whatever else
# was being changed that day. It is free right now and it never will be again.
#
# What it is for: the target is Windows and CI runs on Linux, so the tree is
# checked out under both conventions routinely. Without normalization a Windows
# checkout produces a whole-tree CRLF diff against the Linux history. Doing it here
# rather than via each machine's core.autocrlf means the rule travels with the
# repo instead of depending on every clone being configured correctly.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Baseline
#
# Normalize to LF in the repository, check out native in the working tree. Git
# decides text-vs-binary per file; sections 5 overrides that where the guess is
# not safe enough to rely on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*                       text=auto


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Source and docs
#
# diff=cpp gives hunk headers showing the enclosing function, which makes review
# of the render loop and the analysis stage substantially easier to follow.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.c                     text diff=cpp
*.h                     text diff=cpp
*.cc                    text diff=cpp
*.cpp                   text diff=cpp
*.cxx                   text diff=cpp
*.hpp                   text diff=cpp
*.hxx                   text diff=cpp
*.inl                   text diff=cpp

*.cmake                 text
CMakeLists.txt          text
CMakePresets.json       text
*.json                  text
*.yml                   text
*.yaml                  text
*.md                    text diff=markdown
*.txt                   text
.clang-format           text
.clang-tidy             text
.editorconfig           text
.gitattributes          text
.gitignore              text


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Files that must be LF even on Windows
#
# A shebang line followed by CRLF makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose
# name ends in a carriage return, which fails with a uselessly obscure error.
#
# Crystal source is forced to LF for a different reason: a crystal is a .frag
# plus its .toml manifest, and any shader cache keyed on source content must
# produce the same key on the Windows target and on Linux CI. Both halves are pinned --
# pinning only the shader would still let an identical crystal hash differently
# across platforms.
#
# THE MANIFEST HALF IS PINNED BY EXTENSION, NOT BY PATH, AND THAT IS THE FIX
# FOR A BUG THIS FILE ALREADY HAD.
#
# It used to read `vault/**/*.toml`. There is no vault/ directory -- crystals
# live in crystals/ -- so the rule matched nothing, and manifests fell through to
# the `* text=auto` baseline while the shaders beside them were correctly pinned.
# Exactly the split this comment warns against, live in the tree for months.
#
# CI could not have caught it: the hygiene job checks that committed files match
# their attributes, and a rule matching no files is vacuously satisfied. Nor was
# it visible in a diff -- the committed blob was already LF via text=auto; it was
# the WORKING TREE that differed, CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux, which is
# precisely where a cache would read it.
#
# Naming an extension rather than a directory means the rule cannot drift out of
# step with the layout again, which is how it broke the first time. Nothing in
# this project wants a .toml with CRLF.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.sh                    text eol=lf
*.bash                  text eol=lf
*.zsh                   text eol=lf
*.py                    text eol=lf
configure               text eol=lf
Makefile                text eol=lf

*.frag                  text eol=lf
*.vert                  text eol=lf
*.geom                  text eol=lf
*.comp                  text eol=lf
*.tesc                  text eol=lf
*.tese                  text eol=lf
*.glsl                  text eol=lf
*.inc                   text eol=lf
*.toml                  text eol=lf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Files that must be CRLF even on Linux
#
# cmd.exe misparses a batch file with LF line endings, in ways that depend on
# where the line break falls. There is no upside to normalizing these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.bat                   text eol=crlf
*.cmd                   text eol=crlf
*.sln                   text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj               text eol=crlf
*.props                 text eol=crlf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Binary - never normalize, never diff as text
#
# Git's own text detection looks for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes and
# nothing else. There is no non-printable-ratio heuristic. A binary file that
# happens to contain no NUL in its header -- which is entirely possible for
# audio, font and compressed formats -- is therefore classified as text by
# `* text=auto` above and gets CR bytes injected on a Windows checkout. The file
# is silently corrupted with no error and no warning. These lines are what
# prevent that, so treat the list as load-bearing rather than decorative.
#
# Every pattern is written in character-class form, e.g. *.[fF][lL][aA][cC].
# This is not stylistic. Attribute matching is case-SENSITIVE when
# core.ignorecase is false, which is every Linux checkout and all of CI -- so a
# plain `*.flac` silently fails to match `TRACK.FLAC`. On the Windows target
# core.ignorecase is true and the case folds, so a gap here is invisible exactly
# where the work happens. The corruption path runs the other way: a file added
# from a case-sensitive checkout -- CI, or a contributor on Linux -- misses the
# attribute, gets normalized as text, and arrives corrupt on the target.
#
# ADDING A FORMAT: add it in class form, and mirror it in .gitignore's media
# block so the two files keep describing the same set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Audio - test fixtures the AudioFrame analysis is validated against
*.[aA][aA][cC]          binary
*.[aA][iI][fF]          binary
*.[aA][iI][fF][fF]      binary
*.[aA][pP][eE]          binary
*.[cC][aA][fF]          binary
*.[dD][fF][fF]          binary
*.[dD][sS][fF]          binary
*.[fF][lL][aA][cC]      binary
*.[mM]4[aA]             binary
*.[mM][kK][aA]          binary
*.[mM][pP]3             binary
*.[oO][gG][aA]          binary
*.[oO][gG][gG]          binary
*.[oO][pP][uU][sS]      binary
*.[wW][aA][vV]          binary
*.[wW][mM][aA]          binary
*.[wW][vV]              binary

# Images - documentation figures and crystal preview thumbnails
*.[bB][mM][pP]          binary
*.[dD][dD][sS]          binary
*.[gG][iI][fF]          binary
*.[iI][cC][oO]          binary
*.[jJ][pP][eE][gG]      binary
*.[jJ][pP][gG]          binary
*.[pP][nN][gG]          binary
*.[pP][sS][dD]          binary
*.[tT][gG][aA]          binary
*.[tT][iI][fF]          binary
*.[tT][iI][fF][fF]      binary
*.[wW][eE][bB][pP]      binary

# Video - fixtures for the future video facet
*.[aA][vV][iI]          binary
*.[mM][kK][vV]          binary
*.[mM][oO][vV]          binary
*.[mM][pP]4             binary
*.[wW][eE][bB][mM]      binary

# Fonts - the M6 on-screen library UI
*.[eE][oO][tT]          binary
*.[oO][tT][fF]          binary
*.[tT][tT][fF]          binary
*.[wW][oO][fF][fF]      binary
*.[wW][oO][fF][fF]2     binary

# Archives and opaque blobs
*.7[zZ]                 binary
*.[bB][iI][nN]          binary
*.[bB][zZ]2             binary
*.[dD][aA][tT]          binary
*.[gG][zZ]              binary
*.[pP][dD][fF]          binary
*.[rR][aA][rR]          binary
*.[tT][aA][rR]          binary
*.[xX][zZ]              binary
*.[zZ][iI][pP]          binary

# Compiled output - gitignored, but belt and braces against a force-add
*.[aA]                  binary
*.[dD][lL][lL]          binary
*.[dD][yY][lL][iI][bB]  binary
*.[eE][xX][eE]          binary
*.[lL][iI][bB]          binary
*.[oO]                  binary
*.[oO][bB][jJ]          binary
*.[pP][dD][bB]          binary
*.[sS][oO]              binary


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Language reporting
#
# GitHub classifies .frag/.vert as Fortran-ish or unknown otherwise, which makes
# the repo's language bar nonsense once the vault has any size to it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.frag                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.vert                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.geom                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.comp                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.tesc                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.tese                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.glsl                  linguist-language=GLSL
*.inc                   linguist-language=GLSL

third_party/**          linguist-vendored
docs/**                 linguist-documentation


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Source archives
#
# Affects `git archive` only, which is what backs GitHub's release tarballs and
# "Download ZIP". It does NOT affect `git clone` -- contributors still get these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.gitattributes          export-ignore
.gitignore              export-ignore
.editorconfig           export-ignore
.github/                export-ignore
